      "quality": "poor",
      "qualityReason": "location_description"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Early iron mining in Raubsville began in the 1700s, with ore shipped mainly to Durham Iron Works in Bucks County.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.lehighvalleyhistory.com/the-history-of-raubsville/",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Hematite deposits stretch the full length of the township, yielding thousands of tons annually for nearby furnaces.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8,
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.lehighvalleyhistory.com/the-history-of-raubsville/",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The Glendon Iron Company, founded in the 19th century, still operates several historic mines in the area.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10,
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.lehighvalleyhistory.com/the-history-of-raubsville/",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "A notable feature of the local ore beds is that no pumping is required; horses historically hauled the ore to the surface.",
